# The build's launcher check, one probe per depth

Date: 2026-11-26
Host: Linux x86-64, AMD Ryzen 7 3800X, no GPU and no libcuda
Toolchain: nvcc 12.8.93 from `.validation/cuda-toolchain` (NVIDIA's Ubuntu 24.04
packages, unpacked without root), CMake from `.venv/bin`
Status: **[V]** for the check itself (four builds, one per branch), **[U]** for
anything the launchers would do on a device - they are still never launched.

## What was wrong

`build.sh` counted one name and called any hit "present":

    launcher_syms="$(nm "$ofx_file" | grep -c 'LaunchGainRGBA32F' || true)"
    if [[ "${launcher_syms:-0}" -gt 0 ]]; then
      echo "  CUDA launcher: present (${launcher_syms} symbol(s))"
    else
      echo "  CUDA launcher: ABSENT (CPU-only build)"
    fi

The descriptor declares three depths, so a module can lose the 16F or the 8-bit
launcher and still answer this check with "present". Proof: the doctored module in
the first control below - which has no 8-bit launcher at all - prints
`2 symbol(s)` for that count (the definition and its relocation entry).

## What replaces it

One word-boundary probe per name, all three required of a build that used a
toolkit, and the output says which ones were found:

    launchers=(LaunchGainRGBA32F LaunchGainRGBA16F LaunchGainRGBA8)
    for launcher in "${launchers[@]}"; do
      hits="$(printf '%s\n' "$symbols" \
        | grep -c -E "(^|[^[:alnum:]_])${launcher}([^[:alnum:]_]|$)" || true)"
      ...

The word boundary matters: a symbol that merely starts with a launcher name
(`LaunchGainRGBA8Alt`) must not satisfy the probe for `LaunchGainRGBA8`. The
`grep -c` (not `-q`) keeps the existing SIGPIPE note true - `-q` exits early, `nm`
dies of SIGPIPE and `pipefail` turns a match into a failure.

## Branches, each run

### 1. Complete CUDA build (expected: pass, all three named)

    $ ./templates/minimal-cuda-ofx/build.sh
    ...
    Bundle: .../MinimalCudaGain.ofx.bundle
      .../Contents/Linux-x86-64/MinimalCudaGain.ofx
      exported dynamic symbols: 2 (expected 2)
      CUDA launchers: all 3 present (32F, 16F, 8-bit)
        found: LaunchGainRGBA32F LaunchGainRGBA16F LaunchGainRGBA8
    exit 0

### 2. Negative control: the 8-bit launcher renamed away (expected: fail)

A copy of the template in `/tmp/launcher-control`, with `LaunchGainRGBA8` renamed
to `LaunchGainRGBA8Alt` in `Kernel.cu`, `Kernel.cuh` and `Plugin.cpp` (one
occurrence each), built against the already-fetched openfx checkout
(`OFX_SOURCE_DIR`) so nothing was downloaded:

    $ nm <doctored>.ofx | grep -c LaunchGainRGBA32F
    2                                    # the old check: "present"

    $ ./build.sh
      exported dynamic symbols: 2 (expected 2)
      CUDA launchers: INCOMPLETE - 2 of 3
        found:   LaunchGainRGBA32F LaunchGainRGBA16F
        missing: LaunchGainRGBA8
    error: the module is missing a launcher for a depth its descriptor declares: LaunchGainRGBA8
    exit 1

This control is also the word-boundary test: `LaunchGainRGBA8Alt` is in the symbol
table, and the probe still reports `LaunchGainRGBA8` as missing.

### 3. Negative control: no launcher at all, toolkit present (expected: fail)

`/tmp/launcher-control2`, every `LaunchGain…` renamed to `GainLaunch…`, same
toolkit on `PATH`:

    $ ./build.sh
      exported dynamic symbols: 2 (expected 2)
      CUDA launchers: none found, although a toolkit was used
    error: CUDA toolkit .../cuda-12.8 was used, but the module has no launcher - the device object did not link in
    exit 1

This is the branch that used to say "ABSENT (CPU-only build)" for what may be a
broken CUDA build; the message now distinguishes the two cases.

### 4. `CPU_ONLY=1`, no toolkit (expected: pass, says why)

    $ CPU_ONLY=1 ./templates/minimal-cuda-ofx/build.sh
      exported dynamic symbols: 2 (expected 2)
      CUDA launchers: none found (CPU-only build)
    exit 0

## What this does not cover

- The check reads the symbol table. It does not prove a launcher calls the right
  kernel or runs on a device: no GPU in this cell, so that stays **[U]**
  (`cuda-nvcc-compile.log` covers compilation, `CUDA launcher` here covers
  presence).
- The Windows path (`build.bat` -> `testkit/check-ofx-artifact.bat --cuda`) was
  not changed and not run here.
